<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pax Romana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/27-bce">27 BCE</a>–<a href="/years/180">180</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Roman Peace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pax Romana, the Roman Peace, was a period of roughly 200 years of relative peace, stability and prosperity in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, though it still saw internal revolts and external wars. It is traditionally dated from the accession of Augustus in 27 BCE to the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> in 180 CE. The empire reached its greatest extent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> in 117, and its population is estimated to have peaked at 70 million. Historians debate its limits: Arthur M. Eckstein finds the Roman peace emerging in parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> under the Republic, while Walter Goffart doubted that peace is what the period shows.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pax-romana.jpg" alt="Pax Romana" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carin (empereur romain). Autorité émettrice de monnaie présumée Numérien (0254?-0284; empereur romain). Autorité émettrice de monnaie présu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Aurelianus,_Carin_et_Numérien,_Lyon_-_btv1b113442700_(2_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>For most historians, the Pax Romana began when Octavian, later called Augustus, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Cleopatra at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium">Battle of Actium</a> and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a>, or first citizen. Lacking a good precedent for successful one-man rule, Augustus created a junta of the leading military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> and stood as its front man; by binding them into a coalition he removed the prospect of civil war. Peace did not come at once, since fighting continued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Augustus nonetheless closed the Gates of Janus, a ceremony that indicated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was at peace, in 29 BCE and again in 25 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> regarded peace not as an absence of war but as a rare condition that existed when all opponents had been beaten down. Augustus had to persuade them that the prosperity of peace was better than the wealth of a risky war, and he did so by skilful propaganda. Later emperors staged lavish ceremonies to close the Gates of Janus, issued coins with Pax on the reverse and patronised literature that praised the peace. The first known record of the term is in a writing by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-the-younger">Seneca the Younger</a> in 55 CE. The last five emperors of the period are known as the Five Good Emperors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Historians differ over what the period shows. Walter Goffart observed that the Cambridge Ancient History volume called The Imperial Peace is not a record of peace. Arthur M. Eckstein, by contrast, holds that the Roman peace emerged in large regions much earlier, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> after 210 BCE and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> after 133 BCE. Thomas F. Madden traces its true origins to the 2nd century BCE. The idea proved influential: medieval writers tried to imitate it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-and-truce-of-god">Peace and Truce of God</a>, and historians have coined variants such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-britannica">Pax Britannica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-mongolica">Pax Mongolica</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pax Romana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His sole reign is commonly thought to mark the end of the <b>Pax Romana</b>; Cassius Dio saw in it a descent &quot;from a kingdom of gold to one of iron and rust&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-17">17 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
